 gets $16 per hour? Yeah, that seems right. So, then his second job rate is $16 per hour. \n\nThen he works 30 hours at his main job. So, that's 30 x $20. Let me calculate that. 30 times 20 is... 600. So, $600 from the main job.\n\nThen, he works half that much at his second job. Half of 30 hours is 15 hours. So, 15 x $16. Let me compute that. 15 times 10 is 150, 15 times 6 is 90, total 240. So, $240 from the second job.\n\nTherefore, total weekly earnings would be $600 from the first job plus $240 from the second job. Adding those together: 600 + 240 is... 840. So, $840.\n\nWait, but let me check again if I did the second job correctly. Maybe I made a mistake there. So, original rate at the second job is $20 per hour, then discounted by 20%. So, 20% less is 20% of 20, which is 4, subtracted. So, 20 - 4 = 16. Correct. Then 15 hours at 16 per hour... 15*16. Let me do 16 times 10 is 160, 16*5 is 80, total 240. Correct. So that seems right.\n\nSo, total earnings: 30 hours at $20, plus 15 hours at $16. 30*20 is 600. 15*16 is 240. 600+240=840. So, he earns $840 weekly.\n\nBut just to double-check, maybe I can check the percentages again. Wait, 20% less. So, if instead, maybe he is getting 20% of the rate? But no, wait. Receiving 20% less is a reduction, not a percentage increase. So, yes, 20% off. So, correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe someone could think that 20% less is that 20% more than the previous rate? But that's not how it's phrased. The problem says \"20% less while working his second job.\" So, discount. So, 20% of the original 20%, which is 4, so 20-4=16. Correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe another way: 20% of 20 is 4, so subtract 4, which gives 16. Correct. So, then 16 is the hourly rate. \n\nTherefore, the total earnings are 30 hours at $20 (600) plus 15 hours at $16 (240). Total 840. \n\nWait, is there another way to compute it? Let me think. Maybe breaking down the $240. So, 15 hours at 16.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the second job is half the time? Wait, the problem says: \"He works 30 hours at his main job and half that much at his second job.\" So, 30 hours total, half that much the second job? So, half of 30 is 15. So, that is correct. \n\nSo, 15 hours at second job. So, 15*16 is 240. Then 30*20 is 600. 600+240=840. \n\nTherefore, the answer should be $840, which is put into a box.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{840}\n</think>\n\nJames earns $20 per hour at his main job, and 20% of that amount is reduced at the second job. We need to calculate how much he earns per week.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Calculate Earnings from the First Job (Main)\nJames works 30 hours at his main job:\n\n$$\n30 \\text{ hours} \\times \\$20/\\text{hour} = \\$600\n$$\n\n### Step 2: Calculate Earnings from the Second Job (At 20% Less)\nThe second job rate is 20% less than the main job rate.\n\n1. 20% of $20 = $4.\n2. So, the first job rate at the second job is:\n   $$\n   20 - 4 = \\$16\n   $$\n\nHe works half the number of hours at the second job, which is half of 30 hours:\n\n$$\n\\frac{30}{2} = 15 \\text{ hours}\n$$\n\n$$\n15 \\text{ hours} \\times \\$16/\\text{hour} = \\$240\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Total Weekly Earnings\n\n$$\n\\$600 \\text{ (from first job)} + \\$240 \\text{ (from second job)} = \\$840\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer\n\n$$\n\\boxed{840}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "James earns $20 an hour while working at his main job.  He earns 20% less while working his second job.  He works 30 hours at his main job and half that much at his second job.  How much does he earn per week?", "answer": "James earns 20*.2=$<<20*.2=4>>4 less while working his second job\nSo he earns 20-4=$<<20-4=16>>16 an hour\nAt his first job he earns 20*30=$<<20*30=600>>600\nHe works 30/2=<<30/2=15>>15 hours at his second job\nSo he earns 15*16=$<<15*16=240>>240\nSo he earns 600+240=$<<600+240=840>>840 a week\n#### 840", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: James earns $20 an hour while working at his main job.  He earns 20% less while working his second job.  He works 30 hours at his main job and half that much at his second job.  How much does he earn per week?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["840"], "solution": "840"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 7801, 63759, 400, 17, 15, 458, 6460, 1393, 3238, 518, 806, 1887, 2618, 13, 220, 1260, 63759, 220, 17, 15, 4, 2686, 1393, 3238, 806, 2086, 2618, 13, 220, 1260, 4278, 220, 18, 15, 4115, 518, 806], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 752, 1430, 311, 7071, 700, 1246, 1753, 7801, 63759, 817, 2003, 13, 2055, 11, 566, 4278, 1378, 6887, 25, 806, 1887, 2618, 323, 806, 2086, 2618, 13, 4710, 5338, 11, 1077, 752, 53999, 279, 3491, 13, 1260, 63759, 400, 17, 15, 817, 6460, 518, 806, 1887, 2618, 13, 5005, 11, 1393, 3238, 806, 2086, 2618, 11, 566, 63759, 220]}
