f there are 420 cards, and each page holds 10, then 10 times 42 is exactly 420. So there are no leftover cards. Therefore, he doesn't need to buy an extra page. \n\nAlternatively, I can think of it as (60 packs x 7 cards per pack) / 10 cards per page. Which is same as 420 / 10 = 42. So, yeah, 42 pages. \n\nIs there any chance that the problem expects something else? Like maybe considering the number of pages needed for each pack? But no, since each pack already has 7 cards, and each page has 10. So even if there are partial pages, since we are dividing up the total cards, 420 divided by 10 is exact. \n\nWait, but let me check again. If the total number of cards is 420, and each page can take 10, then yes, 42 pages. Seems straightforward. \n\nI don't see any mistakes here. Maybe another way to check is to see how many cards per page. If he has 420 cards, how many pages? Each page 10. 420 divided by 10 is 42. Yep, that's right. \n\nSo the answer should be 42. So putting it in boxed notation, \\boxed{42}.\n</think>\n\nPunching the math problem, it's clear that Punger needs to collect cards by 60 packs, with each pack containing 7 cards. Hence, the total number of cards is calculated as:\n\n$$\n60 \\times 7 = 420\n$$\n\nEach page can hold 10 cards, so the number of pages needed is calculated by dividing the total number of cards by the number of cards per page:\n\n$$\n\\frac{420}{10} = 42\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total number of pages needed is $\\boxed{42}$.<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Punger collects baseball cards.  He buys 60 packs of baseball cards. Each pack has 7 cards inside. He wants to put these cards in special pages to protect the cards. Each page can hold 10 cards. How many pages does he need to buy?", "answer": "60 packs of cards * 7 cards per pack = <<60*7=420>>420 cards\n420 cards / 10 cards per page = <<420/10=42>>42 pages\n#### 42", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Punger collects baseball cards.  He buys 60 packs of baseball cards. Each pack has 7 cards inside. He wants to put these cards in special pages to protect the cards. Each page can hold 10 cards. How many pages does he need to buy?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["42"], "solution": "42"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 30436, 1389, 51207, 19507, 7411, 13, 220, 1260, 49531, 220, 21, 15, 30306, 315, 19507, 7411, 13, 8886, 3769, 702, 220, 22, 7411, 4766, 13, 1260, 6801, 311, 2182, 1493, 7411, 304, 3281, 6816, 311, 6016, 279], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 752, 1430, 311, 7071, 700, 1246, 1657, 6816, 30436, 1389, 3880, 311, 3695, 13, 2055, 566, 49531, 220, 21, 15, 30306, 315, 19507, 7411, 11, 1817, 3769, 702, 220, 22, 7411, 11, 323, 1817, 2150, 646, 3331, 220, 16, 15, 7411, 13, 88190, 11, 773, 279, 3491, 374, 911, 37614, 1246, 1657, 6816, 566, 3880, 11, 1290, 1939, 5338]}
