2026-10-30 07:47:45 INFO areno.api.trainers.policy_only.PolicyOnlyTrainer policy_only.py:723 - rollout_completion={"kind": "rollout", "epoch": 0, "step": 20, "prompt_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Michelangelo has 28 ceilings to paint. This week, he paints 12 of them. Next week, he will paint 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week. How many ceilings will be left to paint after next week?\nSolution:", "decoded_prompt": "<|im_start|>user\nSolve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Michelangelo has 28 ceilings to paint. This week, he paints 12 of them. Next week, he will paint 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week. How many ceilings will be left to paint after next week?\nSolution:<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n", "completion": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. So Michelangelo has 28 ceilings to paint. This week, he paints 12 of them. Then next week, he paints 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week. I need to find out how many ceilings are left to paint after next week. \n\nFirst, I should break down the problem into steps. Let me start by calculating how many ceilings he paints next week. The problem says he paints 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week. Since this week he paints 12 ceilings, then 1/4 of 12 should be 3. So next week, he will paint 3 ceilings. \n\nWait, let me double-check. If 12 is the amount he paints this week, then 1/4 of 12 is indeed 3. So that seems right. \n\nNow, subtract the number he painted this week from the total ceiling count. Total ceilings are 28, and he painted 12 this week, so the remaining ones would be 28 - 12. Let me calculate that: 28 - 12 is 16. \n\nBut hold on, then next week, he paints 1/4 of what he did this week. Wait, is that a separate thing? Wait, does the problem mean that next week he paints 1/4 of the original number, or does it mean 1/4 of the number he's going to paint this week? \n\nWait, the problem says \"Next week, he will paint 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week.\" So, he will paint 1/4 of the 12 ceilings he did this week. Which again is 3. But then, after that, the total is 28 - 12 = 16, and then subtracting another 3 would make it 13? But that seems like the answer might be different. Wait, let me check again. \n\nWait, the initial total is 28. After this week's 12, remaining is 16. Then next week, he paints 1/4 of the 12. So next week he paints 3. Therefore, after next week, the remaining is 16 - 3 = 13. \n\nBut hold on, is there another interpretation? Maybe he paints 1/4 of the total ceilings this week, which is 12, so 3. But the problem says '1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week'. So the same as before. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is phrased as \"next week, he will paint 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week\", so the 1/4 refers to what? The number he painted this week? \n\nYes, so if this week it was 12, then 1/4 of 12 is 3. \n\nSo total after next week is 16 (previous after this week) minus 3, which is 13. Therefore, the answer should be 13. \n\nBut just to make sure, let me verify again. \n\nOriginal total: 28\n\nWeek 1: 12\n\nWeek 2: 1/4 of 12 = 3\n\nSo total remaining after week 2: 28 - 12 - 3 = 13. \n\nTherefore, the answer is 13, which in LaTeX is \\boxed{13}. \n\nI think that's it. No mistakes here.\n</think>\n\nAfter calculating the number of ceilings Michelangelo paints this week (12), we find that 1/4 of 12 is 3. \n\nThe initial total number of ceilings is 28, so the remaining ceilings after week 1 are:\n\n$$\n28 - 12 = 16\n$$\n\nWith 3 more ceilings painted next week, the remaining ceilings after week 2 are:\n\n$$\n16 - 3 = 13\n$$\n\nThus, the number of ceilings left to paint after next week is $\\boxed{13}$.<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Michelangelo has 28 ceilings to paint. This week, he paints 12 of them. Next week, he will paint 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week. How many ceilings will be left to paint after next week?", "answer": "After this week, Michelangelo will have 28-12=<<28-12=16>>16 more ceilings to paint.\nNext week, Michelangelo will paint 12/4=<<12/4=3>>3 ceilings.\nMichelangelo will have 16-3=<<16-3=13>>13 more ceilings to paint.\n#### 13", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Michelangelo has 28 ceilings to paint. This week, he paints 12 of them. Next week, he will paint 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week. How many ceilings will be left to paint after next week?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["13"], "solution": "13"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 44406, 87910, 702, 220, 17, 23, 55714, 311, 6177, 13, 1096, 2003, 11, 566, 56848, 220, 16, 17, 315, 1105, 13, 9295, 2003, 11, 566, 686, 6177, 220, 16, 14, 19, 279, 1372, 315, 55714, 566, 1521], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 594, 1490, 13, 2055, 44406, 87910, 702, 220, 17, 23, 55714, 311, 6177, 13, 1096, 2003, 11, 566, 56848, 220, 16, 17, 315, 1105, 13, 5005, 1790, 2003, 11, 566, 56848, 220, 16, 14, 19, 279, 1372, 315, 55714, 566, 1521, 419, 2003, 13, 358, 1184, 311, 1477, 700, 1246, 1657, 55714, 525, 2115, 311, 6177, 1283, 1790, 2003]}
