y 2/3. Let me do that. 15 times 2 is 30, divided by 3 is 10. So that means she gave 10 cookies to her sister. \n\nTherefore, starting from 15, giving 10 to her sister would leave her with 15 - 10 = 5 cookies. Hmm, but hold on, let me check if I did everything correctly. \n\nFirst, initial cookies: 20. Subtract 10 to brother: 10 remaining. Then half of that: 10/2 = 5, so total now is 15. Then two-thirds of 15: 15*(2/3) = 10. Subtract that from 15: 5 left. So total left is 5. \n\nBut wait, is there another way to check this? Let's add up all the steps again. \n\nStart: 20\n\n- 10 to brother: 10 cookies left.\n\nThen, mother gave half of that: half of 10 is 5. So total now 10 + 5 = 15.\n\nThen she gave two-thirds to her sister. 2/3 of 15 is 10. So subtract 10: 15 - 10 = 5 left.\n\nSo, yes, 5 cookies left. \n\nBut wait, maybe I made a mistake in fractions. Let me confirm. 2/3 of the number. So yes, 15*(2/3) is indeed 10. \n\nAnother way: Let's do the entire equation. \n\nTotal cookies at start: 20.\n\nCookies given to brother: 10. So cookies remaining: 20 - 10 = 10.\n\nCookies given to mother (half of brother's cookies given): half of 10 is 5. So total after that: 10 + 5 = 15.\n\nCookies given to sister (two-thirds of her own): 15*(2/3) = 10. So remaining: 15 - 10 = 5.\n\nYes, same result. \n\nSo, the answer is 5. So, in the box, put \\boxed{5}.\n</think>\n\nSabrina starts with 20 cookies. She gives 10 cookies to her brother, leaving 10 cookies. Her mother then halves the amount given to her brother, resulting in 5 additional cookies. Sabrina gives two-thirds of her remaining cookies to her sister, which is $ \\frac{2}{3} \\times 15 = 10 $, leaving 5 cookies. \n\n$$\n\\boxed{5}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Sabrina gave 10 cookies to her brother. Her mother gave Sabrina half the number of cookies she gave her brother. Then Sabrina gave two-thirds of her cookies to her sister. If Sabrina had 20 cookies at the start, how many cookies are left with her?", 