 10,000 divided by 500 is indeed 20. So, he needs 20 tanks. \n\nIs there any chance I messed up the multiplication or division? Let me check the math again. 10,000 divided by 500. If I divide both numerator and denominator by 100, it becomes 100 divided by 5, which is 20. Yep, that's correct. So the answer should be 20 tanks.\n</think>\n\nJohn buys 1000 balloons, each with 10 liters of air. To fill all the balloons, the total volume needed is $1000 \\text{ balloons} \\times 10 \\text{ liters/balloon} = 10,000 \\text{ liters}$. \n\nEach 500-liter tank provides 500 liters. To find out how many tanks are required, we divide the total volume by the capacity of a tank:\n\n$$\n\\frac{10,000 \\text{ liters}}{500 \\text{ liters/tank}} = 20\n$$\n\nThus, he needs to buy $\\boxed{20}$ tanks.<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "John buys 1000 balloons.  Each balloon holds 10 liters of air.  If he buys 500-liter tanks of gas, how many tanks does he need to buy to fill all the balloons?", "answer": "He needs 1000*10=<<1000*10=10000>>10000 liters of gas\nSo he needs 10000/500=<<10000/500=20>>20 tanks\n#### 20", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: John buys 1000 balloons.  Each balloon holds 10 liters of air.  If he buys 500-liter tanks of gas, how many tanks does he need to buy to fill all the balloons?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["20"], "solution": "20"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 3757, 49531, 220, 16, 15, 15, 15, 69480, 13, 220, 8886, 46756, 9982, 220, 16, 15, 92866, 315, 3720, 13, 220, 1416, 566, 49531, 220, 20, 15, 15, 85594, 25866, 315, 6819, 11, 1246, 1657, 25866, 1558], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 594, 1430, 311, 7071, 700, 419, 3491, 13, 2055, 3757, 49531, 220, 16, 15, 15, 15, 69480, 11, 323, 1817, 46756, 646,