o more than three times the number of cows We the People has.\" So that would be 3 times We the People's cows plus two. So that would be 3*17 + 2. Let me check that again. If they have two more than three times, then yes, it's 3*17 + 2. So 51 + 2 is 53. So Happy Good Healthy Family has 53 cows?\n\nWait, but maybe my understanding is wrong. Because sometimes \"more than\" can mean something else. Like, if the original amount was 17, then \"two more\" would mean 17 + 2 = 19. But here it's \"three times the number of cows\" so maybe they are adding two to triple the number.\n\nSo which one is it? The problem says \"two more than three times the number of cows We the People has.\" So mathematically, that would be 2 + 3*(17). Let me compute that again: 3*17 is 51, plus 2 is 53. Yes, so Happy Good Healthy Family has 53 cows.\n\nThen if their cows are taken to a ranch to graze together, how many cows will be in the ranch altogether? So we need to add both numbers: 17 (We the People) and 53 (Happy Good Healthy Family). So 17 + 53. Let me check that sum. 17 + 50 is 67, and then +3 is 70. So total is 70 cows.\n\nWait, hold on. Let me check all steps again.\n\nWe the People: 17\n\nHappy Good Healthy Family: 3 times 17 is 51, plus 2 is 53. Correct.\n\nTotal cows: 17 + 53.\n\n17 + 50 is 67, plus 3 is 70. So 70.\n\nSo the answer should be \\boxed{70}.\n</think>\n\n\\boxed{70}<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "We the People has 17 cows. Happy Good Healthy Family has two more than three times the number of cows We the People has. If their cows are taken to a ranch to graze together, how many cows will be in the ranch altogether?", "answer": "Three times the number of cows We the People has is 3*17=<<3*17=51>>51 cows.\nHappy Good Healthy Family has two more than three times the number of cows We the People has, a total of 51+2=53 cows.\nIf the cows are taken to a ranch to graze together, their total will be 53+17=<<53+17=70>>70 cows.\n#### 70", "prompt": "Solve the followin