00 = 400? But let me double-check. The initial schools had 15, added five more, so total classes are 15 + 5 = 20. But does that affect the number of students? Because if they added the new classes, each of those new classes already have students. Wait, the problem says \"they added five more classes\" so does that mean they added students to the existing ones or spread them out? Hmm, the wording is a bit ambiguous. But in most school problems like this, when they mention \"added five more classes\", they mean that each of those classes is full, but maybe they already had students. But the problem says \"they added five more classes\", so I think it's safe to assume that the existing classes are still there and the new classes are also newly established with students. Therefore, the number of students is calculated as 15 classes times 20 students, plus 5 classes times 20 students. So that would be 15*20 + 5*20. Which is (15+5)*20 = 20*20 = 400. So the total number of students is 400, so \\boxed{400}.\n\nBut let me check once more. Initially, 15*20=300. Adding five more classes, each with 20 students, so 5*20=100. Total students would be 300 + 100 = 400. Yep, that seems right. I don't see any other factors here. The problem doesn't mention any changes in student numbers or anything else, just states the initial number of students and adds new classes. So the answer should be 400.\n</think>\n\n\\boxed{400}<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Texas Integrated School has 15 classes and has 20 students per class.  They added five more classes, how many students will they have now?", "answer": "The school has 15 x 20 = <<15*20=300>>300 students right now.\nThey have an additional 5 x 20 = <<5*20=100>>100 students after adding 5 more classes.\nTherefore, Texas Integrated School will have a total of 300+100= <<300+100=400>>400 students.\n#### 400", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nP