wed money 6 months ago, and now she is able to return half of the money. So, perhaps at the current time, she has half of the money she borrowed, and she has 4 months left until then (since the time from now to 4 months ahead is 4 months). Therefore, we need to find out how much she still owes 4 months from now.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is that she borrowed money and is repaying for 6 months now, and then she is going to continue repaying for another 4 months, so total of 10 months incurring the debt.\n\nBut maybe another approach is needed.\n\nLet me define variables. Let’s let the original total amount borrowed be $A. Shara borrowed money from her brother 6 months ago. She returned $10 per month. So, for the 6 months she borrowed, she has returned 6*10 = $60. So, she planted the initial amount A, and then she started repaying $10 per month for 6 months.\n\nNow, currently, she is able to return half of the money she borrowed. Therefore, she has half the total amount A left.\n\nBut how is this related to the repayment?\n\nWait, repayment is happening continuously? Or does it refer to the current time?\n\nWait, perhaps the problem is that Shara borrowed money and started repaying for 6 months. Now, at the current time (which is now + 10 months?), she is able to return half of the amount she borrowed. So, perhaps there was an initial loan, and she started repaying for 6 months, then now she has some money, and she has half of X, meaning she has repaid back X, and now she still owes X/2.\n\nThis is getting confusing. Maybe I need to set up equations.\n\nLet me try again.\n\nLet’s suppose the original amount borrowed was $X. 6 months ago, she had borrowed X, and for each month she returns $10. So, over 6 months, she started repaying $10 * 6 = $60. Therefore, she has R1 = X - 60. But at the current time, she has half of the original amount, so half of X. Therefore, R1 = X/2. So, X - 60 = X/2. Solving for X: X = 60 * 2 = $120. Therefore, total amount borrowed i