gons are already given as 3. The lilies are twice as many. So if in one arrangement there are 3 snapdragons, then lilies are 3 times something? Wait, maybe the wording is confusing here.\n\nWait, maybe it's that the number of lilies is twice the number of snapdragons. So if snapdragons are 3, then lilies would be 6. That makes sense. So lilies are twice as many as snapdragons. So once again, lilies: 3 snapdragons, so twice that would be 6 lilies.\n\nSo the arrangement per centerpiece has: roses (8), daisies (12), snapdragons (3), lilies (6). So total per centerpiece is 8+12+3+6= 29 flowers. Then since she needs to arrange this for 10 tables, the total number of flowers needed would be 29 multiplied by 10, which is 290.\n\nWait, but hold on. Let me check again. The problem says \"arrangements\" for a reception with 10 tables. So maybe there are multiple centerpieces, but each arrangement is just that one. So if each arrangement is 29 flowers, then 10 arrangements would be 10*29=290. So the answer is 290. But maybe I made a mistake here. Let me check once more.\n\nOriginal problem: Each arrangement has 8 roses, 12 daisies, 3 snapdragons, and twice as many lilies. So lilies are twice as many as snapdragons? Wait, is it twice as many lilies as the number of snapdragons? Let me parse the sentence again: \"Each arrangement needs to have 8 roses, 12 daisies, 3 snapdragons and twice as many lilies.\" So \"twice as many lilies\" – does \"twice as many\" refer to lilies being twice the number of snapdragons? If so, then lilies would be 2 * snapdragons? Which would make sense. So snapdragons are 3, so lilies would be 6. So yes, lilies are twice as many as snapdragons? So 3*2=6 lilies. Therefore, each arrangement has 8+12+3+6=29 flowers, and total for 10 tables is 29*10=290. Therefore, the answer is \\boxed{290}.\n</think>\n\n$$\n\\boxed{290}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Marta is arranging floral centerpieces for a reception party.  Each arrangement needs to have 8 roses,12 daisies, 3 snapdragons and twice as many lilies.  The reception will have 10 tables.  How many flowers will she need in total to fill this order?", "answer": "She needs twice as many lilies as the 3 snapdragons in the arrangement so she needs 2*3 = <<2*3=6>>6 lilies\nShe needs 8 roses, 12 daisies, 3 snapdragons and 6 lilies for a total of 8+12+3+6 = <<8+12+3+6=29>>29 flowers per arrangement\nShe needs 29 flowers per each of the 10 tables for a total of 29*10 = <<29*10=290>>290 flowers\n#### 290", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Marta is arranging floral centerpieces for a reception party.  Each arrangement needs to have 8 roses,12 daisies, 3 snapdragons and twice as many lilies.  The reception will have 10 tables.  How many flowers will she need in total to fill this order?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["290"], "solution": "290"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 8127, 64, 374, 76118, 45019, 4126, 46468, 369, 264, 23117, 4614, 13, 220, 8886, 26112, 3880, 311, 614, 220, 23, 60641, 11, 16, 17, 294, 2782, 550, 11, 220, 18, 10658, 33931, 2382, 323, 10917, 438, 1657], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 594, 1490, 13, 8127, 64, 3880, 311, 30893, 45019, 4126, 46468, 369, 220, 16, 15, 12632, 13, 8886, 26112, 702, 220, 23, 60641, 11, 220, 16, 17, 294, 2782, 550, 11, 220, 18, 10658, 33931, 2382, 11, 323, 10917, 438, 1657, 326, 7597, 13, 88190, 11, 773, 1156, 11, 358, 1184, 311, 7071, 700, 1246, 1657, 326, 7597, 1340]}
