2026-10-30 08:24:31 INFO areno.api.trainers.policy_only.PolicyOnlyTrainer policy_only.py:723 - rollout_completion={"kind": "rollout", "epoch": 0, "step": 57, "prompt_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Five food companies sponsored a local food bank. Foster Farms donated 45 dressed chickens; American Summits donated twice the number of bottled water than the number of dressed chicken donated by Foster Farms; Hormel donated three times the number of dressed chickens that Foster Farms donated; Boudin Butchers donated one-third of the number of dressed chickens that Hormel donated; Del Monte Foods donated 30 fewer bottles of water than American Summits. How many food items did the companies donate in total?\nSolution:", "decoded_prompt": "<|im_start|>user\nSolve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Five food companies sponsored a local food bank. Foster Farms donated 45 dressed chickens; American Summits donated twice the number of bottled water than the number of dressed chicken donated by Foster Farms; Hormel donated three times the number of dressed chickens that Foster Farms donated; Boudin Butchers donated one-third of the number of dressed chickens that Hormel donated; Del Monte Foods donated 30 fewer bottles of water than American Summits. How many food items did the companies donate in total?\nSolution:<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n", "completion": "<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out this problem. So there are five food companies that donated some food items, and I need to find the total. Let me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, let me list out all the donated items per company and see the relationships between them. \n\nStarting with Foster Farms: They donated 45 dressed chickens. \n\nThen American Summits donated twice the number of bottled water than the number of dressed chickens. Since Foster Farms donated 45, which is the number of dressed chickens, American Summits must have donated 45 multiplied by 2. Let me check that: 2*45 = 90. So American Summits donated 90 bottled water.\n\nNext, Hormel donated three times the number of dressed chickens that Foster Farms donated. Foster Farms had 45, so Hormel donated 3*45. Let me calculate that: 3*45 is 135. Therefore, Hormel donated 135 dressed chickens.\n\nBoudin Butchers donated one-third of the number of dressed chickens that Hormel donated. Hormel had 135, so Boudin Butchers donated 1/3 of that. 135 divided by 3 is 45. So Boudin Butchers donated 45 dressed chickens.\n\nFinally, Del Monte Foods donated 30 fewer bottles of water than American Summits. American Summits had 90 bottled water, so Del Monte Foods has 90 - 30 = 60 bottles of water.\n\nNow, to find the total number of food items donated by all companies, I need to add up the dresses chickens and the bottled water.\n\nSo let's list all the donations for each company:\n\n- Foster Farms: 45 dressed chickens\n- American Summits: 90 bottled water\n- Hormel: 135 dressed chickens\n- Boudin Butchers: 45 dressed chickens\n- Del Monte Foods: 60 bottled water\n\nAdding them all together:\n\nDressed chickens: 45 (Foster) + 135 (Hormel) + 45 (Boudin) = 45 + 135 is 180, plus 45 is 225.\n\nBottled water: 90 (A.S.) + 60 (D.L.) = 150.\n\nTotal food items: 225 (chickens) + 150 (water) = 375.\n\nSo the total number of food items donated is \\boxed{375}. That should be the answer.\n</think>\n\nThe total number of food items donated by all companies is calculated by summing up the contributions from each company:\n\n- Foster Farms contributes 45 dressed chickens.\n- American Summits contributes $2 \\times 45 = 90$ bottles of water.\n- Hormel contributes $3 \\times 45 = 135$ dressed chickens.\n- Boudin Butchers contributes $1/3 \\times 135 = 45$ dressed chickens.\n- Del Monte Foods contributes $90 - 30 = 60$ bottles of water.\n\nAdding these together:\n- Chickens: $45 + 135 + 45 = 225$\n- Water: $90 + 60 = 150$\n\nTotal food items: $225 + 150 = \\boxed{375}$.\n\n$$\n\\boxed{375}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Five food companies sponsored a local food bank. Foster Farms donated 45 dressed chickens; American Summits donated twice the number of bottled water than the number of dressed chicken donated by Foster Farms; Hormel donated three times the number of dressed chickens that Foster Farms donated; Boudin Butchers donated one-third of the number of dressed chickens that Hormel donated; Del Monte Foods donated 30 fewer bottles of water than American Summits. How many food items did the companies donate in total?", "answer": "American Summits donated 45 x 2 = <<45*2=90>>90 bottled waters.\nHormel donated 45 x 3 = <<45*3=135>>135 spams.\nBoudin Bakery donated 135 x 1/3 = <<135*1/3=45>>45 sourdoughs.\nDel Monte Foods donated 90 - 30 = <<90-30=60>>60 canned fruits.\nTherefore, a total of 45 + 90 + 135 + 45 + 60 = <<45+90+135+45+60=375>>375 different food.\n#### 375", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Five food companies sponsored a local food bank. Foster Farms donated 45 dressed chickens; American Summits donated twice the number of bottled water than the number of dressed chicken donated by Foster Farms; Hormel donated three times the number of dressed chickens that Foster Farms donated; Boudin Butchers donated one-third of the number of dressed chickens that Hormel donated; Del Monte Foods donated 30 fewer bottles of water than American Summits. How many food items did the companies donate in total?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["375"], "solution": "375"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 20924, 3607, 5110, 29538, 264, 2205, 3607, 6073, 13, 38174, 6